<h2>A prosecution that unravelled</h2>
<p>The case against the petitioner was circumstantial from the outset, and the Supreme Court’s audit of it reads like a checklist of how not to build one. He was not named in the FIR. He was not named in the statements recorded during investigation under Section 161 CrPC. The ransom demand said to supply the motive for the kidnapping was never reported to the police while the investigation was alive — it surfaced for the first time in the courtroom. Six prosecution witnesses were examined, and none of the familiar circumstantial anchors held.</p>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li><strong>Electronic evidence:</strong> the CCTV footage and call detail records were not proved with the certificate the law mandates for electronic records, and the footage itself was unclear.</li>
<li><strong>Recoveries:</strong> no disclosure statement under Section 27 of the Evidence Act was recorded, and no witness deposed that the accused led the police to any recovery site.</li>
<li><strong>Forensics:</strong> the charred bones recovered were never identified as those of the missing person, and the post-mortem could not conclusively establish the cause of death.</li>
<li><strong>Confession:</strong> the confession allegedly made to the police was unreliable, and the last-seen circumstance was not effectively proved.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>On this record the acquittal was, as the Court’s reasoning makes plain, the only outcome the evidence permitted. What set the judgment apart is that the Court refused to let the matter end there.</p>

<h2>"Six years erased from his life"</h2>
<p>The petitioner had been in custody since 14 November 2019. The Supreme Court, seized of his special leave petition, had directed the trial court to conclude the trial within three months — a direction that produced the acquittal. Surveying the whole course of the case, the Bench observed: <strong>"It is unfortunate that despite no evidence against the petitioner herein, he was imprisoned for six years as an undertrial."</strong> It deprecated the manner in which both the investigation and the prosecution were conducted, describing the former as slipshod and the latter as haphazard.</p>
<p>The operative directions followed from that finding. The State of Uttar Pradesh was directed to pay the petitioner <strong>Rs 6 lakh</strong> as compensation. And in a pointed institutional message, the Court directed that a copy of the order be sent to the Director General of Police and the Home Secretary of the State, remarking that <em>"the State Police would do well to educate their officers in the process of investigation."</em></p>

<h2>Where this fits in the law of wrongful incarceration</h2>
<p>India has no general statutory scheme compensating persons acquitted after long incarceration; what exists is the public-law jurisdiction of constitutional courts to award monetary compensation where the State’s machinery has violated Article 21. Judgments of this kind operate within that jurisdiction: the award is not an assessment of civil damages but a vindication of the liberty interest, fixed in a round sum against the State. The present decision is notable for pairing the award with named institutional recipients — the DGP and the Home Secretary — so that the censure lands where corrective action can follow.</p>
<p>For the defence bar, the judgment is also a working illustration of how Article 136 can be used. The special leave petition here did not merely contest an order; it became the vehicle through which the Supreme Court monitored the trial timeline, received the outcome, and then moulded relief to the injustice the record disclosed. Where a client has spent years in custody on visibly deficient evidence, the petition should place the whole investigative record before the Court — the gaps in the FIR, the unproved electronic material, the barren recoveries — because it is that cumulative picture which moves a court from acquittal to accountability.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The judgment does not convert every acquittal into a compensation claim. The award rested on a custody of six years traceable to an investigation the Court found indefensible. But it confirms that where those elements coincide, the Supreme Court is prepared to say so in terms — and to make the State pay.</p>
</div>
